It seems like simple encryption system has been implemented,
where every character exept firsth is pushed one, than two than three... and so one
characters ahead in ASCI table. I figured it out by typing in "aaa" in the form,
which was turned in to "abc". To confirm it i tryed "(((" which was encrypted into "()*"



Basicaly encryption of string depends which character in string it is and it determinates
how far it is pushed ahead in ASCI table.

a is nr 97 in ASCI table
b is nr 98 in ASCI table
c is nr 99 in ASCI table


[0] (97)  a -> (97 + 0 = 97)  a
[1] (97)  a -> (97 + 1 = 98)  b
[2] (97)  a -> (97 + 2 = 99)  c





Same checked out with special characters

( is nr 40 in ASCI table
) is nr 41 in ASCI table
* is nr 42 in ASCI table


[0] (40)  ( -> (40 + 0 = 40)  (
[1] (40)  ( -> (40 + 1 = 41)  )
[2] (40)  ( -> (40 + 2 = 42)  *



It's clear that to revert encryption we must do oposite to given string. Mine is: "98f3=8?7"

[0] (57)  9 -> (57  -  0 = 57)  9
[1] (56)  8 -> (56  -  1 = 55)  7
[2] (102) f -> (102 -  2 = 100) d
[3] (51)  3 -> (51  -  3 = 48)  0
[4] (61)  = -> (61  -  4 = 57)  9
[5] (56)  8 -> (56  -  5 = 51)  3
[6] (63)  ? -> (63  -  6 = 57)  9
[7] (55)  7 -> (55  -  7 = 48)  0

So I ended up with password: 97d093930


It took me around 1 minute to manualy write it down looking at ASCII table...
So i decided to spend additional 3 minutes on coding it,
to automate the task i'll most likely never have to do ever again.

Here is a simple python script

```
encrypted = "98f3=8?7"
y = 0
ASCII = []



for i in encrypted:
    ASCII.append(ord(i) - y)
    y += 1



decoded = "".join(map(chr,ASCII))

print(decoded)
```
